{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\nOn the Gerrit `Verified` gate, `tests/unit/test_opcert_service_app.py` intermittently crashes its pytest-xdist\nworker (`worker gwN crashed`; `[gwN] node down: Not properly terminated`), failing the whole matrix job and\nproducing a spurious `Verified -1`. The failure is a hard worker-process death (not an assertion), lands on a\nRANDOM matrix shard per run (~1 of 4), and is timing/platform-dependent. Observed 4+ consecutive times on\n2026-08-08 blocking change 1545 (ticket ad16), whose diff is markdown/comment-only and cannot touch\n`opcert_service`. Reproduction is CI-only (never locally).\n\n## Root cause\nThe background worker offloads each job with `asyncio.to_thread(jobs.run_job, ...)`\n(`src/rebar/opcert_service/app.py` ~line 77), which runs on the **event loop's global default\n`ThreadPoolExecutor`**. The lifespan teardown (`app.py` ~55-59) cancels and awaits the worker *asyncio task*,\nbut `asyncio.to_thread` exposes no handle to the underlying OS thread, and that thread's disposal is tied to the\nloop's `shutdown_default_executor()`. When the `TestClient` anyio portal tears the fixture down under xdist, the\nloop/portal can close without deterministically disposing the default executor, so a leftover default-executor\nthread is still unwinding when the worker process exits -> `[gwN] node down: Not properly terminated`. The\npreviously-proposed \"make the `with TestClient(...) as c:` exit guaranteed\" change is a no-op (the fixture at\n:44-49 already uses a `with` block).\n\n## Constraint: this must stay consistent with ADR-0067\n`review_bot/app.py`, which this service mirrors, DELIBERATELY keeps `asyncio.to_thread` and accepts the\nun-force-cancellable orphan thread; ADR-0067 (\"review-bot bounded shutdown\") explicitly **rejects an unbounded\n`await`/join at teardown** because a genuinely hung `run_job` would hang shutdown forever. So the fix must NOT\nintroduce a blocking `executor.shutdown(wait=True)` join (that reopens ADR-0067's rejected alternative). The\nchange below is additive: it preserves the bounded/abandon-in-flight property and only makes the executor's\nper-loop disposal DETERMINISTIC.\n\n## Plan (app-owned executor, bounded disposal — `src/rebar/opcert_service/app.py`)\n- In `lifespan` startup, create an app-owned executor:\n  `app.state.executor = concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=WORKER_COUNT, thread_name_prefix=\"opcert-job\")`.\n- In `_worker`, replace `asyncio.to_thread(jobs.run_job, ...)` with\n  `loop.run_in_executor(app.state.executor, functools.partial(jobs.run_job, ...))` (same call, same\n  `asyncio.wait_for(..., job_timeout_seconds)` wrapper, same result/timeout semantics — no request/response\n  behavior change).\n- In the lifespan `finally`, AFTER the existing bounded task cancel/await, dispose the app-owned executor with a\n  BOUNDED, NON-blocking shutdown: `app.state.executor.shutdown(wait=False, cancel_futures=True)`. This cancels\n  queued futures and lets idle worker threads exit on the sentinel, WITHOUT blocking on an in-flight job\n  (ADR-0067-consistent: abandon in-flight, the process is exiting). Because the executor is owned by the app and\n  disposed INSIDE the lifespan shutdown (which `TestClient` runs before it closes the loop/portal), its threads\n  are handled deterministically at teardown instead of leaking onto the loop's global default executor.\n\nExplicitly REJECTED alternatives (documented so they are not re-litigated):\n- `@pytest.mark.xdist_group` co-location — INERT here: the default suite that runs this unit module uses\n  `--dist worksteal` (`.github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml:653-654`); `xdist_group` only groups under\n  `--dist loadgroup`, which CI uses ONLY for the `-m integration` tier (line 668). Switching the unit lane's\n  dist-mode edits contended CI config, which is out of this epic's non-overlapping scope.\n- `executor.shutdown(wait=True)` blocking join — reopens ADR-0067's rejected unbounded-shutdown alternative.\n\n## Testing\nDeterministic, in-session-evaluable checks (no probabilistic CI dependence, no undeclared tooling):\n- Teardown disposes the app-owned executor (the actual end-state that removes the orphan): a unit test enters the\n  `TestClient` context, exercises a job round-trip, exits the context, then asserts the app-owned executor is\n  shut down (`app_module.app.state.executor._shutdown is True`) and no thread whose name starts with\n  `opcert-job` remains alive\n  (`[t for t in threading.enumerate() if t.name.startswith(\"opcert-job\") and t.is_alive()] == []`).\n- The offload no longer uses the loop's global default executor:\n  `grep -n \"asyncio.to_thread\" src/rebar/opcert_service/app.py` returns `0` matches, and\n  `grep -n \"run_in_executor\\|ThreadPoolExecutor\\|executor.shutdown(wait=False, cancel_futures=True)\" src/rebar/opcert_service/app.py`\n  shows the app-owned executor, its use, and its bounded disposal.\n- Existing HTTP-plumbing tests still pass: `.venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/unit/test_opcert_service_app.py -q`.\n- CI corroboration (monitored, NOT the acceptance oracle since it is probabilistic): the `Verified` matrix shows\n  no `worker gwN crashed` / `node down` line for this module after the change.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `src/rebar/opcert_service/app.py` offloads `jobs.run_job` to an app-owned `ThreadPoolExecutor`\n      (`app.state.executor`, `thread_name_prefix=\"opcert-job\"`) via `run_in_executor`, not `asyncio.to_thread`\n      (`grep -n \"asyncio.to_thread\" src/rebar/opcert_service/app.py` returns 0 matches).\n- [ ] The lifespan `finally` disposes that executor with a BOUNDED, non-blocking shutdown consistent with\n      ADR-0067 (no `wait=True` join):\n      `grep -n \"executor.shutdown(wait=False, cancel_futures=True)\" src/rebar/opcert_service/app.py` returns a\n      match and `grep -n \"shutdown(wait=True\" src/rebar/opcert_service/app.py` returns 0 matches.\n- [ ] A deterministic teardown test in `tests/unit/test_opcert_service_app.py` proves the orphan is gone: after\n      the `TestClient` context exits, `app.state.executor._shutdown is True` and no live thread's name starts\n      with `opcert-job`.\n- [ ] `.venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/unit/test_opcert_service_app.py -q` passes (all existing HTTP-plumbing\n      tests plus the new teardown test).\n- [ ] The change is ADR-0067-consistent: it adds no unbounded join and removes no shutdown budget; applying the\n      same refinement to `review_bot/app.py` is explicitly out of scope (its own ticket)."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786239869535159001,"uuid":"f2c2c36a-64fc-490f-892f-3d6c7b2a4401"}